    "instructions": {
        "Presentations" : "<p> Students will work on a term project throughout the semester and present that project over three talks. </p>  <h2>Deliverable</h2> <p> By Sunday evening of the week of a presentation, students should submit their project in its most recent state as a single compressed (.zip) email attachment to the instructor. </p>    <h2>Talk</h2> Each talk will feature two primary, interwoven components: <ol style='padding-left:2.25em'> <li> <b>Demo.</b> Prepared demonstration / explanation. The student prepares this ahead of time. Counts towards the grade. </li> <li><b>QnA.</b> Dynamic question answering. Instructor initiated at various points during the demo. Student response assessed and considered toward grade assignment.</li> </ol> </p>      <p> Students will be in front of the class for equivalent amounts of time during each presentation. What will vary between students is the amount of time spent on <i>demo</i> versus <i>QnA</i>. Demos that are informative and engaging will likely involve / necessitate less QnA, so it is in a student's best interest to make the demo portion(s) as informative and engaging as possible.</p>  <p><h2>Durations</h2> <style> .durations-table { border-collapse: collapse; margin-left:2.25em; } .durations-table th, .durations-table td { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding-left: 6px; padding-right: 6px; padding-top: 6px; padding-bottom: 6px; } .durations-table th { background: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; } .durations-table tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: #fafafa; } </style> <p> <table class='durations-table'> <thead> <tr> <th>Presentation</th> <th>Duration</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>1: The Pitch</td> <td>6 min</td> </tr> <tr> <td>2: The Progress</td> <td>8 min</td> </tr> <tr> <td>3: The...Perfection?</td> <td>10 min</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </p> </p> <br> <h2>Rubric</h2> <p> <style> .rubric-table { border-collapse: collapse; margin-left: 2.25em; } .rubric-table th, .rubric-table td { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding-left: 6px; padding-right: 6px; padding-top: 6px; padding-bottom: 6px; } .rubric-table th { background: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; } .rubric-table tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: #fafafa; } </style> <table class='rubric-table'> <thead> <tr> <th></th> <th>2 pts</th> <th>1 pt</th> <th>0 pts</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Deliverable</strong> truency</td> <td> </td> <td>On time.</td> <td>Late.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Deliverable</strong> progress</td> <td> At or exceeding.</td> <td>Some work, but lagging.</td> <td>Well behind.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Demo</strong> representation</td> <td>Totally matches deliverable.</td> <td>Somewhat matches deliverable.</td> <td>Doesn't match deliverable.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Demo</strong> clarity</td> <td>Very clear.</td> <td>Somewhat clear.</td> <td>Not clear.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Demo</strong> engagement</td> <td>Very engaging</td> <td>Somewhat engaging.</td> <td>Not engaging.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>QnA</strong> accuracy</td> <td>Answers fully correct.</td> <td>Partially but mostly correct.</td> <td>Frequently incorrect responses.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>QnA</strong> clarity</td> <td>Answers completely clear.</td> <td>Mostly but not entirely clear.</td> <td>Answers pretty unclear.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>QnA</strong> ownership</td> <td>Definitive ownership.</td> <td>Somewhat questionable ownership.</td> <td>Highly questionable ownership.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </p> <br>    <h2>Notes</h2> <ol style='padding-left:2.25em'> <li>Time limits are firm. Practice with a timer.</li> <li>Presentation order will be randomly determined by instructor ahead of time.</li> <li>Have a plan B for demo failures (recording, second machine, etc). Recovering well counts.</li> <li>Slides/notecards fine. Scripts and pre-loaded AI answers are not.</li> </ol> </section>",
        "Progress reports" : "<p>On weeks we don't have a project presentation, you'll turn in a short, <b>handwritten</b> progress report. This is due at the start of class, on paper: notebook or printer paper is fine. Legibility and presentation is important and will be graded. </p> <p> <p><h2>Head</h2> <ol style='margin-left:2.25em'><li>Your name</li><li>Due date</li><li>Topic(s) covered during the prior week.</li></ol></p>    <p><h2>Body</h2> <ol style='margin-left:2.25em'><li>What you got done on your project since your last report.</li><li>How you worked the piror week's concept(s) into your project. Be specific: don't just say \"I added loops,\" tell me what they're actually doing in your code and why you built it that way.</li><li>Anything you're stuck on or still figuring out.</li></ol></p>   <p><h2>Notes</h2> <ol style='margin-left:2.25em'><li>Honesty is valued. If you barely touched the project one week, say that and explain what got in the way. An honest short report is better than a vague long one.</li><li>Specific detail that shows meaningful understanding is essential to earn full points, not vague descriptions or padded length.</li></ol></p>",
        "Semester project" : "<p>Students will work on a single large project throughout the semster. They will build it continuously from the second week of class through until the last week. Each time a new concept is introduced in class, students are expected to work that concept into their project in a way that is correct, meaningful, and observable.</p> <p><h2>What to build</h2>Pick something you'll actually want to show people or use. You are going to be spending a lot of time on it, and the more excited you are by the idea, the more enjoyable your experience will be, and the better the project will be.</p> <p><h2>Assessment</h2>There are two primary assessment modes:<ol style='padding-left:2.25em'> <li><b>Progress reports.</b> Most weeks, there will be a progress report due.</li> <li><b>Presentations.</b> At three points throughout the semester, you will give a presentation on your work.</li></ol></p> <p><h2>Progress reports</h2>Every week (after the first week) that we <i>don't</i> have a presentation, you'll turn in a short written progress report covering what you did and how you integrated the <i>prior</i> week's concept(s) into your final project.</p> <p><h2>Presentations</h2>Three times throughout the semester, you'll present your project to the class.<ol style='padding-left:2.25em'> <li><b>Presentation 1. The Pitch.</b> What you are building and why, plus whatever you have so far.</li> <li><b>Presentation 2. The Progress.</b> Show significant progress with your project.</li> <li><b>Presentation 3. The...Perfection?</b> Show the final state of the project (which won't be perfect, and that's okay).</li></ol></p> <p><h2>What success looks like.</h2>Overall, a highly successful project will be:<ol style='padding-left:2.25em'> <li><b>Coherent.</b> The components of the project fitt together in a way that makes sense. There aren't random for loops that do nothing that were added just so the project had for loops, for instance.</li> <li><b>Complete.</b> Clearly uses everything we covered during the semseter.</li> <li><b>Smooth.</b> Runs cleanly, without hiccups.</li> <li><b>Valuable.</b> Does something genuinely useful or is really fun to interact with. No one needs a janky calculator app that they have to access through a terminal.</li></ol></p>"
